<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Popayán</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1537">1537</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Popayán is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, capital of the Cauca <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, founded by Sebastián de Belalcázar in 1537. In the colonial era it was a wealthy seat of landowners and mine owners whose gold mines in the Pacific lowlands were worked by enslaved Africans, and it produced many leaders of the independence era. Known as the 'White City' for its whitewashed colonial buildings, it is famous for its Holy Week processions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/popayan.jpg" alt="Popayán" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Junior Segura fotografía · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atardecer_en_Popay%C3%A1n,_Cauca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Popayán lies in the Pubenza Valley in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, between two mountain ranges, at 1,760 metres above sea level, with an average temperature of 18 °C. The city is known as the white city for the colour of its colonial buildings and as the University City, since the University of Cauca, founded in 1827 by decree of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a>, is based there. The origin of the name is uncertain: one theory reads it as two villages with reed roofs, another derives it from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> named Payán, and a third from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> for the valley of the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nothing is recorded of the pre-Hispanic village of Popayán. El Morro del Tulcán, a truncated pyramid built between 500 and 1600 CE, was already abandoned when the Spanish arrived, and analysis of dental samples suggests that those buried there probably belonged to the upper class. The conquistador Sebastián de Belalcázar arrived on 13 January 1537. In the 16th and 17th centuries Popayán was governed by an appointed governor under the Royal Audience of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>.</p><p>Its position between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena</a> made it important, and even after the Pacific was reached it remained a transfer point for gold and other riches going to Cartagena on their way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It also served as a colonial mint, producing gold escudo coins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> reales from 1760 through 1819, and it continued to strike coins for the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> after 1826. A battle for independence was fought at the country house of Calibio on 15 January 1814. During that struggle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-narino">Antonio Nariño</a> had the lead weights of the cathedral clock tower's English clock, installed in 1737, changed to supply metal for ammunition. In 1873 the Humilladero Bridge was built over a fault, and it has stayed intact through many earthquakes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Popayán is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Cauca, with a municipal population of 318,059 and an area of 483 square kilometres. It has been the home of seventeen Colombian presidents. An earthquake of 31 March 1983, lasting eighteen seconds, toppled many of the city's buildings; reconstruction took more than ten years, and the quake led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>'s first seismic design code. The historic centre keeps its baroque architecture and its churches, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> and the cathedral. In 2005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> named Popayán its first city of gastronomy, and in 2009 it declared the Easter Week processions of Semana Santa a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Patrimony of Humanity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Popayán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its economy depended on gold from Antioquia, <b>Popayán</b> and the Chocó, mined increasingly by enslaved Africans, as well as emeralds from Muzo.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-kingdom-of-granada">New Kingdom of Granada</a></li></ul></section>
